## Usage
{% if project_type == "cli" %}
```bash
uv run {{ project_slug }} info       # run the CLI
uv run {{ project_slug }} --version  # print the version
python -m {{ package_name }}         # equivalent module entry point
```

Or use it as a library:
{% endif %}
```python
from {{ package_name }}.logging import get_logger, setup_logging
from {{ package_name }}.settings import load_settings

settings = load_settings()
setup_logging(settings.logging, as_json=settings.app.environment == "production")
log = get_logger(__name__)
log.info("started", environment=settings.app.environment)
```

## Configuration

Startup reads environment variables and model defaults. No dotenv files or secrets directories are discovered automatically. Load settings once in your entry point and pass the relevant groups to the modules that need them; library callers control loading and logging themselves.

For local overrides, copy the committed `.env.example` (checked against the settings model) to a gitignored `.env`:

```bash
cp .env.example .env
```

{% if project_type == "cli" %}
Select it before the subcommand:

```bash
uv run {{ project_slug }} --env-file .env info
```
{% endif %}
In Python:

```python
from pathlib import Path

from {{ package_name }}.settings import load_settings

settings = load_settings(env_file=Path(".env"))
```

Settings are resolved with the following precedence, highest first:

1. Explicit field overrides passed to the `Settings` constructor
1. Real environment variables (`APP__APP_NAME=...`)
1. The explicitly selected dotenv file
1. Files in the explicitly selected secrets directory
1. Defaults declared in `src/{{ package_name }}/settings.py`

Deployment can supply environment variables or mounted secret files. To read `/run/secrets/APP__SECRET_KEY`, call `load_settings(secrets_dir=Path("/run/secrets"))`{% if project_type == "cli" %} or run `uv run {{ project_slug }} --secrets-dir /run/secrets info`{% endif %}. Secret filenames use the same nested names as environment variables. Both explicit sources can be selected together.

Relative paths, including dotenv paths, secrets directories, and log paths, are relative to the process working directory. Use absolute paths when launching from another directory. Explicit missing configuration paths fail at startup. `.env.local` has no special meaning; it is read only if selected instead of `.env`.

Unknown keys in an env file are ignored, so `.env` can be shared with other tooling, but a misspelled `APP__`/`LOGGING__` key fails at startup.

## Logging

`setup_logging(settings.logging, as_json=...)` sends JSON to the configured log file and uses the requested console format. The CLI selects JSON for production and human-readable output otherwise. Library logs are rendered through the same pipeline, so everything in the file has the same shape.

```python
from {{ package_name }}.logging import get_logger

log = get_logger(__name__)
log.info("order placed", order_id=42, total="19.99")
```

## Development

```bash
uv run pytest                       # run tests
uv run pytest --cov                 # run tests with the coverage gate
uv run pytest -m "not slow"         # skip the slow ones
uv run ruff check .                 # lint
uv run ruff format .                # format
uv run ty check                     # type check
uv run pre-commit run --all-files   # the full gate{% if ci != "none" %}, which is what CI runs{% endif %}
```

Test order is randomized on every run (`pytest-randomly`). A failure prints the seed; replay it with `uv run pytest -p randomly --randomly-seed=<seed>`.
{% if ci == "github" %}

### CI

[`.github/workflows/ci.yml`](.github/workflows/ci.yml) runs the pre-commit suite and the test suite on Ubuntu and Windows, builds the docs with `--strict`, and audits dependencies weekly. The hook suite is the single source of truth, so CI cannot drift from what runs locally.

[`.github/dependabot.yml`](.github/dependabot.yml) opens a weekly pull request for the pinned action versions and another for the pre-commit hook revs; Python dependencies stay on `uv lock --upgrade`.
{% elif ci == "gitlab" %}

### CI

[`.gitlab-ci.yml`](.gitlab-ci.yml) runs the pre-commit suite and the test suite, builds the docs with `--strict`, and audits dependencies. The hook suite is the single source of truth, so CI cannot drift from what runs locally.

Two things need setting up in the GitLab UI: a pipeline schedule with `SCHEDULED_AUDIT` set to `1`, for the weekly dependency audit, and — if the docs should be published — a run of the manual `pages` job.
{% endif %}

### Docs

```bash
uv sync --group docs                # install docs dependencies
uv run mkdocs serve                 # serve docs locally at http://127.0.0.1:8000
uv run mkdocs build --strict        # build static site into site/ (as CI does)
```
